## 5. The steering layer (project memory)

Steering files are loaded as project memory on every interaction. They define **what the agent must know without being told**.

### 5.1 Foundational files

Three files form the baseline. Every project SHALL have them.

**`product.md`** — purpose, users, value.

```markdown
---
inclusion: always
---

# Product

## Purpose
[One-paragraph description of what this product does and for whom.]

## Core Capabilities
- [3-5 key capabilities, not exhaustive]

## Target Use Cases
[Primary scenarios the product serves.]

## Value Proposition
[Why this product exists.]
```

**`tech.md`** — stack and standards.

```markdown
---
inclusion: always
---

# Technology

## Architecture Approach
[High-level pattern: monolith, microservices, event-driven, etc.]

## Core Technologies
- Language: [version]
- Framework: [name and version]
- Runtime: [name and version]

## Key Libraries
[Only major libraries that shape the codebase.]

## Development Standards
- Type Safety: [policy]
- Code Quality: [linter, formatter]
- Testing: [framework, coverage targets]

## Key Technical Decisions
[Architectural decisions with rationale — not exhaustive.]
```

**`structure.md`** — organization and naming.

```markdown
---
inclusion: always
---

# Structure

## Organization Philosophy
[How the codebase is organized and why.]

## Directory Patterns
| Location | Purpose | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `src/domain/` | Business logic | `src/domain/orders/` |

## Naming Conventions
- Files: [convention]
- Components: [convention]
- Functions: [convention]

## Import Organization
[Path aliases, import order, etc.]

## Code Organization Principles
[Principles for module boundaries, dependency direction, etc.]
```

### 5.2 Custom steering files

Add files for cross-cutting concerns — `security.md`, `api-conventions.md`, `testing.md`, `observability.md`, `frontend-components.md`, `database.md`, `deployment.md`, `performance.md`, `accessibility.md`.

Each uses YAML frontmatter to declare inclusion mode:

```yaml
---
inclusion: always       # always loaded
---

---
inclusion: fileMatch    # loaded when matching files are touched
fileMatchPattern: ["app/api/**/*", "**/*Controller.*"]
---

---
inclusion: manual       # only on demand
---

---
inclusion: auto         # auto-loaded when relevant
name: api-design
description: REST API design patterns. Use when creating or modifying endpoints.
---
```

### 5.3 The steering golden rule

> **If new code follows existing patterns, steering should not need updating.**

Document organizational patterns, naming conventions, import strategies, architectural decisions, and technology standards — **not file listings** or implementation detail. Never include secrets or agent-specific tooling directories.

### 5.4 `security.md` template

```markdown
---
inclusion: always
---

# Security Guidelines

## Principles
- Never log secrets, tokens, passwords, cookies, or personal data.
- Validate inputs at boundaries: API, queues, jobs, external integrations.
- Use least privilege for permissions and credentials.
- Prefer temporary and scoped credentials.
- Do not create authentication/authorization bypasses without explicit approval.

## When modifying code
- Check the impact on authentication, authorization, and data exposure.
- Add tests for access denial and invalid inputs.
- Ensure that errors do not leak internal details.

## Review
- In PRs with security impact, include a "Security considerations" section.
```

### 5.5 `api-conventions.md` template

```markdown
---
inclusion: fileMatch
fileMatchPattern: ["app/api/**/*", "src/api/**/*", "**/*Controller.*", "**/*Route.*"]
---

# API Conventions

- Use plural resource names.
- Return standardized errors with code, message, and correlationId.
- Do not return sensitive data.
- Validate payloads before calling domain services.
- Separate controller/route from business rules.
- Include tests for success, validation, authorization, and external errors.
- Use pagination on lists.
```

### 5.6 Steering best practices

- One domain per file.
- Descriptive name.
- Include the "why", not just the rule.
- Use before/after examples where they clarify.
- Do not include secrets, tokens, credentials, customer data, or sensitive information.
- Review during architecture and planning changes.
- Version with the repository when project-specific.
- Require code review for relevant steering changes.
- Use global steering only for broad team/company policies.

