## 7. Generation rules and review gates

Located in `.kiro/settings/rules/`. These are not advisory — they are checklists the agent runs mechanically before claiming a phase is complete.

### 7.1 Required rule files

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `ears-format.md` | EARS templates, keyword rules, testability checks |
| `requirements-review-gate.md` | Pre-approval checklist for `requirements.md` |
| `design-principles.md` | Type safety, dependency direction, component design, error handling |
| `design-discovery-full.md` / `design-discovery-light.md` | Discovery scope for design phase |
| `design-synthesis.md` | Synthesis rules for design consolidation |
| `design-review-gate.md` | Pre-approval checklist for `design.md` |
| `gap-analysis.md` | Brownfield investigation framework |
| `tasks-generation.md` | Task ordering, annotations, sizing |
| `tasks-parallel-analysis.md` | Parallel-safety rules for `(P)` tasks |
| `steering-principles.md` | What belongs in steering vs not |

### 7.2 Review gate pattern

Every gate file has the same shape:

```markdown
# <Phase> Review Gate

## Scope and Coverage
- [ ] [coverage check 1]
- [ ] [coverage check 2]

## Quality Criteria
- [ ] [quality check 1]
- [ ] [quality check 2]

## Mechanical Checks
- [ ] Numeric IDs are present and unique.
- [ ] Cross-references resolve (e.g., `_Requirements: 1.2_` exists in requirements.md).
- [ ] No implementation language in requirements.
- [ ] File Structure Plan is fully populated.
- [ ] Every component has a Contract Definition.
```

The agent SHALL run the checklist mechanically and report results before the human reviews.

### 7.3 Mechanical vs judgment checks

- **Mechanical checks** are executed by the agent before reporting — ID uniqueness, cross-reference resolution, presence of required sections. These do not require human judgment.
- **Judgment checks** are reviewed by humans after the mechanical checks pass — whether the requirements actually capture intent, whether the design is the right approach, whether the boundaries are well-chosen.

Mixing the two wastes human review time. Mechanical first, judgment second.

