## 20. Anti-patterns to avoid

### 20.1 SDD anti-patterns

- Skipping phase gates with `-y` or `--auto` outside of explicitly fast-track cases.
- Writing tasks as file-level instructions ("edit `albums.ts`") instead of capabilities and outcomes.
- Omitting `_Boundary:_` on `(P)` tasks — silent breakage in parallel runs.
- Allowing `design.md` to grow past 1,000 lines instead of decomposing the spec.
- Treating `research.md` as optional decoration — every finding must connect to a design commitment.
- Using `kiro-spec-quick` for regulated, security-sensitive, or ambiguous domains.
- Letting steering accumulate stale facts — review on every architectural change.
- Treating specs as documentation written *after* the code.
- Mixing exploratory prompting with production-impacting changes in the same session.
- Skipping the "why" — recording rules without the rationale that lets future reviewers judge edge cases.
- Letting specs and code drift silently — without periodic reconciliation, SDD collapses into traditional documentation.

### 20.2 Kiro IDE/operational anti-patterns

- Asking "implement this" without context, requirements, or acceptance.
- Using Autopilot for a critical task without a Spec.
- Trusting all commands/tools for convenience.
- Putting secrets in prompts or steering.
- Committing `mcp.json` with credentials. Use `${ENV_VAR}` placeholders.
- Using Quick Plan for regulated or ambiguous domains.
- Accepting a diff without running tests.
- Mixing large refactoring with functional change.
- Installing Powers/MCPs from unknown sources.
- Keeping steering out of date.
- Treating Supervised mode as a sandbox.
- Using Kiro Web with untrusted repositories or overly broad permissions.

### 20.3 Skill authoring anti-patterns

- Generating a skill from an LLM with no domain context.
- Generic instructions ("handle errors appropriately," "follow best practices") with no concrete behavior.
- Documenting what the agent already knows (HTTP, JSON, common languages).
- Presenting multiple tools as equal options instead of choosing a default.
- Mixing unrelated capabilities into a single skill.
- Skipping iterative refinement — shipping the first draft.
- Storing detailed reference material in `references/` without telling the agent when to load it.
- Treating gotchas as optional polish rather than the core value of the skill.
- Writing prose descriptions of output format instead of providing a template.
- Reinventing deterministic logic in prompt form instead of bundling a script.
- Putting the whole workflow inside one giant skill instead of decomposing into a coherent surface (e.g., the 17-skill set).

